>
  <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Yak Hotpot</h1>
  <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-500">The definitive cold-weather meal of Shangri-La. Tender yak meat slices, local mushrooms, Tibetan herbs, and vegetables simmered in a bubbling spicy broth. Best enjoyed on a chilly highland evening with friends.</p>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">What Makes Yak Hotpot Special?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Yak meat is fundamentally different from beef. Yaks graze on wild alpine grasses and herbs at 3,000&ndash;5,000 meters, developing meat that is <strong>leaner, denser, and richer in iron and omega-3 fatty acids</strong> than domesticated cattle. The meat has a deeper, slightly gamey flavor and remains tender when thinly sliced and quickly cooked in hotpot broth. It does not have the heavy, greasy mouthfeel that some beef hotpots do.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">The broth in Shangri-La yak hotpot is typically a <strong>Tibetan-Sichuan hybrid</strong>: a rich bone broth base infused with Sichuan peppercorns (ma la), dried chilies, ginger, garlic, and local Tibetan herbs like <em>rhodiola</em> (hong jing tian), believed to help with altitude adaptation. Many restaurants offer a split-pot option with one spicy side and one herbal/mild side.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">The hotpot is a social meal. A large pot sits in the center of the table, everyone cooks their own ingredients, and the meal can stretch for two hours. It is the most popular dinner choice in Shangri-La for groups, couples, and solo travelers who find friendly tablemates at their hostel.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Best Yak Hotpot Restaurants in Shangri-La</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 space-y-5">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Dukezong Old Town (Best Atmosphere)</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">The old town has the highest concentration of yak hotpot restaurants. They range from rustic family operations to upscale Tibetan dining halls. A meal for two costs <strong>120&ndash;200 RMB</strong> including meat, vegetables, mushrooms, and drinks. Popular spots cluster around Sifang Street and the lanes leading to the prayer wheel.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Local Favorites Near the Bus Station</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">For a more local (and cheaper) experience, walk north from the old town toward the bus station area. Restaurants here cater to residents rather than tourists. <strong>80&ndash;130 RMB</strong> for two. Less English spoken but pointing at ingredients works perfectly.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-slate-900">Hotel Restaurants</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Upscale hotels like Songtsam and the Shangri-La Resort serve refined yak hotpot in elegant settings. <strong>300&ndash;500 RMB</strong> for two. Good option if you want premium ingredients and English-speaking service.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">What to Order with Your Hotpot</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
      <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold flex-shrink-0">&rarr;</span> <span><strong>Matsutake mushrooms</strong> (July&ndash;September): The ultimate hotpot ingredient. Add them last as they cook in 30&ndash;45 seconds and become rubbery if overdone. Seasonal price addition: 30&ndash;60 RMB per plate.</span></li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold flex-shrink-0">&rarr;</span> <span><strong>Highland barley noodles:</strong> Thick, chewy noodles made from qingke barley. 10&ndash;15 RMB.</span></li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold flex-shrink-0">&rarr;</span> <span><strong>Wild vegetables:</strong> Seasonal mountain greens you won't find elsewhere. The restaurant staff can recommend what's fresh.</span></li>
      <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-600 font-bold flex-shrink-0">&rarr;</span> <span><strong>Tibetan barley wine:</strong> The traditional drink pairing. Served warm in a small pot. 15&ndash;30 RMB.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Where to Buy Yak Meat</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong>Fresh yak meat:</strong> The morning market near Dukezong east gate sells fresh yak meat daily. <strong>50&ndash;70 RMB per 500g</strong>. Look for deep red color with fine marbling.</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600"><strong>Dried yak meat (jerky):</strong> A popular souvenir sold throughout the old town. Flavored with Sichuan pepper, chili, or cumin. <strong>80&ndash;150 RMB per 500g</strong>. The shops near Sifang Street offer vacuum-sealed packages suitable for air travel. Great hiking snack for the Tiger Leaping Gorge trek.</p>
  </section>

